## the k<=7 prefix reproduces exactly, at a different shard count
`catalog_digest.py --diff` between the k=7 run (200 shards) and the k<=7 files of this run (400
shards): IDENTICAL, 6223 blocks, digest aa2cff7bd10c919f. This is the real regression check on the
whole pipeline, and it is stronger than the usual one because the shard count differs — an
order-insensitive digest is the only way to compare across shard counts (`shasum` reports a false
difference; see catalog_digest.py's header).

## 400 shards did NOT lower the parallel floor
Slowest single shard 803 s of 4140 total = 19.4%. At k=7 with 200 shards it was 218 s of 1109 =
19.7%. The fraction is unchanged, so doubling the decomposition bought nothing: ONE first-vertex-type
subtree dominates, and initex() cannot split inside a single first type. The floor is proportional,
not a fixed cost, so it will keep taking ~20% of serial time at every k.
⚑ Getting past it needs depth-2 sharding (split on the first TWO vertex types) — a code change to
initex(), not a knob. That is the next real parallelism win; at k=9 the floor alone will be ~40 min.
⚑ Pooled CPU-s are inflated by 1-second shard-timer granularity over 400 shards; the k<=6 column is
single-core and exact, k>=7 is not directly comparable to it.
